course: dictionary
lesson: 3037
title: Earmuffs and context
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: You can often work out a new word from the words around it. These words are its context, and they give clues. «Woolly mammoths were a bit smaller than an African elephant, with long hair, small ears and curly tusks: small ears and thick fur keep heat in, just as a woolly hat and earmuffs do.» Keeping heat in is the job of something warm, so earmuffs must be warm things for the ears, like a woolly hat.
explain: Earmuffs can also be hard. In safety lessons, the word means ear defenders, which protect your ears from loud noise near machines or fireworks. The same word can mean a soft winter cover or a hard safety cover, so the lesson around the word tells you which one is meant.
explain: Check your meaning by putting it in the sentence in place of the hard word. «Keep heat in, just as a woolly hat and a soft, warm cover for the ears do.» It still makes sense, so you have the right meaning.
fact: An earmuff is a soft, warm cover for one ear.
fact: Earmuffs are usually a pair of these, joined by a band over the head.
fact: Ear defenders are hard earmuffs that protect ears from loud noise.
fact: «A pair of earmuffs» and «fluffy earmuffs» are useful phrases to remember.
fact: Context clues help you tell which meaning of a word is being used.
exercise: «Small ears and thick fur keep heat in, just as a woolly hat and earmuffs do.» What is the job of earmuffs in this sentence? | To help you swim faster | *To keep ears warm | To make ears louder | To hold up your hair
exercise: In a safety lesson, a teacher says ear defenders are earmuffs for loud machines. What are ear defenders? | *Hard earmuffs that protect ears from loud noise | Soft hats for cold winter walks | Bands that go around the waist | Glasses that help you see in the dark
exercise: Which phrase shows that earmuffs come as a pair? | a loud bang | a thick glove | *a pair of earmuffs | a woolly scarf
test: What are earmuffs usually joined by? | A string around the waist | A zip down the back | *A band over the head | A button on the sleeve
test: Why might someone wear earmuffs on a frosty walk? | *To keep their ears warm | To block out the sunshine | To stop their hair falling out | To carry their keys
exam: The word earmuffs in the mammoth sentence means what? | A large animal with curly tusks | *A soft, warm cover for the ears | A loud machine that makes noise | A type of hat for the head
